WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.610
I'd like to now introduce our featured presenter Matt Rothe.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:18.810
Matt is co-founder of the FEED Collaborative at Stanford University where he is

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:21.240
an educa- educator practitioner in

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.450
design thinking and sustainable food system innovation.

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.610
Matt is also a lecturer in

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:30.840
the Graduate School of Business Executive Education program where he frequently

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.524
engages with social entrepreneurs and executives

00:00:33.524 --> 00:00:37.170
seeking to create social and environmental impact in their work.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.975
Prior to his career as a designer and teacher,

00:00:39.975 --> 00:00:42.120
Matt was fellow at the Stanford d. School,

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.395
Director of Stanford Sustainable Food Program and

00:00:45.395 --> 00:00:49.690
Operations Executive at both Attune and Niman Ranch.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:54.955
Raised on a 10,000 acre conventional corn farm on the plains of Colorado,

00:00:54.955 --> 00:00:58.460
Matt's more formal degrees include a BA in

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:00.935
Environmental Earth Science from Dartmouth College and an

00:01:00.935 --> 00:01:04.115
MBA from the Graduate School of Business at Stanford.

00:01:04.115 --> 00:01:07.175
And with that, let's turn it over to Matt.

00:01:07.175 --> 00:01:09.500
All right. Thank you very much Megan.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.080
And, uh, good morning to everyone out there, it's, uh,

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:15.535
wonderful to see all of your smiling in shining faces this morning.

00:01:15.535 --> 00:01:19.265
Um, so I'm looking forward to the conversation today and I think, uh,

00:01:19.265 --> 00:01:20.615
maybe to just get us started,

00:01:20.615 --> 00:01:22.980
I'll point out, um, a couple of things.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:24.570
One is the, the title here,

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.085
You're Asking The Wrong Questions.

00:01:26.085 --> 00:01:28.875
Um, at a high-level, what we're talking about is,

00:01:28.875 --> 00:01:33.215
uh, that most people when thinking about problems and solutions often spend, uh,

00:01:33.215 --> 00:01:37.700
far more time in thinking about whether or not they have the right, uh,

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:42.500
solution in mind and what we've experienced over a decade of

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:47.450
working with entrepreneurs and innovators in and around the food system is that most,

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:49.130
most people in organizations do not spend

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:51.230
nearly enough time really thinking about the problem that,

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:54.680
that they're trying to solve through the work and through the organization.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.945
And so that's what we're gonna be spending our time on today.

00:01:56.945 --> 00:02:01.570
I teach at the Design School here and our goal is always to make,

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:03.890
uh, the teaching and learning that we do as experiential

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:06.650
as possible and I'm gonna attempt to do that today.

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:09.470
And so what I'd like for you to do is if you

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:12.290
have a blank sheet of paper and something with which to write,

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:13.370
uh, in your vicinity,

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:15.720
I'm gonna give you about a minute, uh,

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.315
to find that and if you don't have something with which to write,

00:02:18.315 --> 00:02:20.000
you don't have a place where you have a piece of paper

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.890
available to you but I want you to- to do just to,

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:25.265
uh, think about what I'm asking you here.

00:02:25.265 --> 00:02:26.870
So in the next 60 seconds,

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.765
what I'd like you to do is to identify

00:02:29.765 --> 00:02:33.050
an organizational problem on which you're presently focused.

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:35.780
And I want you to write that down on a piece of paper

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:38.450
or have it in mind, and as you're doing that,

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:41.780
I'd like for you to frame it in a way that I've framed these examples here which

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:45.475
is the problem is that and then fill on the blank to that.

00:02:45.475 --> 00:02:48.740
And so I'm giving you range of examples here of things that, uh,

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.435
people are often thinking about

00:02:50.435 --> 00:02:52.610
problems that they're trying to solve in- in the course of their work.

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:56.360
So I'll give you 60 seconds right now to write the problem that you're

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:57.410
presently focused on in

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:01.470
your organization and then we're gonna come back and we're going to move forward.

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:07.530
Okay. Here we go.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:13.970
So I'm gonna start by talking a little bit about my background as a way to set up, uh,

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:19.489
how I've gotten here and what you see here is a picture of me and my dad on a combine,

00:03:19.489 --> 00:03:23.210
um, on this day, we were combining corn in our 10,000 acre,

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:25.370
uh, farm on the Eastern plains of Colorado.

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:27.650
And I mentioned this because, uh,

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:30.120
as I was approaching graduation from my undergraduate degree,

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:31.200
I recall that my dad,

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.700
having thought a lot about what I wanted to do for a career afterwards.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:39.020
And it occurred to me that farming was really what I wanted to do and so I called

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:43.280
them up and I asked him if I could come back and farm with him an- and I proposed,

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.520
uh, because at the time organic was, uh,

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:50.360
kind of taking off in the marketplace I saw it as an opportunity for our farm.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:54.890
Uh, I proposed to my dad that we begin to convert to,

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:56.985
uh, organic farming, uh,

00:03:56.985 --> 00:03:59.015
and that I come back and help him do that.

00:03:59.015 --> 00:04:02.195
And, uh, I wasn't prepared for the response he gave me.

00:04:02.195 --> 00:04:04.295
Uh, the response was,

00:04:04.295 --> 00:04:06.530
uh, sorry son, uh,

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:11.070
we can do neither of those and I think we were both disappointed b-

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:13.220
by that response but what he explained to me

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:15.595
were a couple of problems with our agriculture,

00:04:15.595 --> 00:04:18.090
um, which, which I'm showing you here.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:20.780
The first one is that with respect to organic,

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:22.400
um, from his mind, you know,

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:26.270
the problem is that farmers need to meet the nutritional demands of a growing population

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:30.800
and he just didn't see a way for organic to do that at the time.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:33.230
And then the second problem that he identified

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:35.570
for me was this problem that with farming is that

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:40.625
the consolidation is making it really hard to pass farms along to the next generation.

00:04:40.625 --> 00:04:44.360
And so really for me this

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:49.430
instigated a career in really trying to think about and solve these problems.

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:51.180
And what I'm gonna share with you today is,

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:54.290
is some of my experience and thinking about these problems.

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:56.750
Um, and where we're at, uh,

00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:59.555
in terms of working on these today and the work that we're doing now.

00:04:59.555 --> 00:05:01.670
More importantly, to think about and share with you

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:04.580
some tools that I've developed along the way that I think will be useful

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:06.740
to you and your organizations as you're thinking about

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:10.460
problems that we're facing today and we've identified just now.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:17.750
So I think just set this up I wanna share a few kind of key learning moments for me,

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:20.370
uh, along the way in terms of how I got to,

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:22.175
to be doing what I'm doing now.

00:05:22.175 --> 00:05:24.860
And the first one it was really, uh,

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:27.080
my initial job out of school working at a ranch

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.915
where in the course of the six years I worked there,

00:05:29.915 --> 00:05:31.940
we were able to, um,

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:35.375
move from supporting 50 small family farmers to 500.

00:05:35.375 --> 00:05:38.570
And for me that became a real example of how we can

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:42.365
scale sustainable agriculture which I then took in to

00:05:42.365 --> 00:05:45.590
Business School here with the mindset of really trying to understand better th-

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:49.070
the economics of farming and how we might re-think,

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:53.005
uh, economic relationships between buyers and sellers.

00:05:53.005 --> 00:05:56.185
And then unfortunately, in the second year of Business School, uh,

00:05:56.185 --> 00:05:59.405
my dad was diagnosed with pancreatic cancer and, um,

00:05:59.405 --> 00:06:03.710
which is essentially a year-long death sentence which it turned out to be.

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:07.170
Uh, but in that, in that year, um,

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:08.955
I ended up helping my dad manage my,

00:06:08.955 --> 00:06:14.360
our farm and then helping my mom in selling the farm after he passed away.

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:18.860
And there were a couple of really critical moments in that experience as terrible as it

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:23.180
was that really redefined how I think about problems generally,

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:26.090
you know, especially within agriculture and I wanna

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:29.500
share with you is that at this moment that occurred for me.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:34.235
Um, and so after my dad passed away as we were dealing with,

00:06:34.235 --> 00:06:36.080
ah, real estate agent to sell the farm,

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:40.055
he had asked me if I could take a look at my dad's yields as a way to

00:06:40.055 --> 00:06:44.270
put a value or help put a value on the farm and so I dug into his files,

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:46.715
and I found, um,

00:06:46.715 --> 00:06:50.540
a graph that looks not unlike this one that which

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:54.290
demonstrating the yields of corn over the course of our,

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:56.240
uh, the history of our farm.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:57.510
And, and what you see here is,

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:00.005
is more or less approximately, um,

00:07:00.005 --> 00:07:04.795
what I had seen which was steadily increasing yields over time.

00:07:04.795 --> 00:07:08.315
And from the perspective of valuing the farm this was, um,

00:07:08.315 --> 00:07:13.885
very helpful because it suggested that the farm is increasing in its value over time.

00:07:13.885 --> 00:07:15.560
And so later that afternoon,

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:17.630
so I was looking at this in the morning, later in the afternoon,

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:18.965
we met with the, um,

00:07:18.965 --> 00:07:23.030
the real estate agent out on the farm to show him around and give him the tour.

00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:25.025
And I'll never forget, uh,

00:07:25.025 --> 00:07:27.955
it was such a poignant moment for me that I took a picture of it.

00:07:27.955 --> 00:07:30.185
An- and this is the picture that I took.

00:07:30.185 --> 00:07:32.550
And so we're sitting on a hill overlooking one of the,

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:35.285
the center pivot irrigation circles on our farm.

00:07:35.285 --> 00:07:39.925
And this was I think June or July and it was after, um,

00:07:39.925 --> 00:07:44.270
what had been the most rain that we'd received in the spring, um,

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:48.350
in reported history I believe and we are in a short, um,

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:51.860
short grass prairie region which is effectively desert,

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:53.960
we get less than 10 inches of rain a year.

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:57.020
And so it's really unusual to see the kind of green

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:00.125
that we're seeing here and it was really a magnificent sight.

00:08:00.125 --> 00:08:05.435
But I was struck by the juxtaposition of that with

00:08:05.435 --> 00:08:11.150
the circle that you see there on the left which is brown and ostensibly dead.

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:14.750
And we went down and we took a closer look at it.

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:17.270
And this is a picture o- of that farm,

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:20.840
this is ostensibly where we're growing food.

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:25.885
And I was just struck by the fact that there is, um,

00:08:25.885 --> 00:08:28.220
there was nothing growing here and I'm pretty

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:31.580
confident that we had sprayed to kill everything.

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:35.595
But what I noticed was that the soil on this field was just,

00:08:35.595 --> 00:08:40.085
um, devoid of life of any kind.

00:08:40.085 --> 00:08:42.380
And so in my mind, you know,

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:47.390
as I was looking at this graph earlier in the day realizing what seemed to

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:49.715
be increasing productivity of the farm

00:08:49.715 --> 00:08:53.015
and then actually going out and seeing it in this state,

00:08:53.015 --> 00:08:57.185
it really occurred to me that something is fundamentally wrong here.

00:08:57.185 --> 00:09:02.060
And I didn't maybe think of this problem that my dad, um,

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:05.240
had mentioned as being kind of the fundamental problem of our agriculture was that

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:11.115
farmers need to meet the nutritional demands of a growing population.

00:09:11.115 --> 00:09:16.700
And it struck for me just question is,

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:19.880
is this the right problem to be solving to the extent

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:23.970
that we are doing to the soil what we appear to be doing?

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:27.320
And so that launched me on to the path that

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:31.250
I am now really rethinking how we approach agriculture.

00:09:31.250 --> 00:09:33.955
I- I want to just

00:09:33.955 --> 00:09:41.440
demonstrate the challenge that we face in thinking about problems and one is what,

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:43.125
what I want to demonstrate here.

00:09:43.125 --> 00:09:46.370
And that none of us growing up as kids in school

00:09:46.370 --> 00:09:49.975
were ever asked the question who knows the right problem.

00:09:49.975 --> 00:09:53.810
Th- the question almost always is who

00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:57.320
has the right answer and so from our very beginnings,

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:02.390
we are patterned on this idea that there's one problem and one solution.

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:06.070
And this continues into our work culture and our work environments.

00:10:06.070 --> 00:10:08.360
And I'm, I'm demonstrating an example here.

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:11.435
This would feel absurd I think to most of us if

00:10:11.435 --> 00:10:14.930
anyone asked us the question that's presented here.

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:18.530
And the reality is that we operate in organizations

00:10:18.530 --> 00:10:22.220
where we are incentivized to design brilliant solutions.

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:26.890
And we're not incentivized to design brilliant problems.

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:33.910
Now, I think that this quote from Einstein really sums up why this matters.

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:36.450
Uh, and I'll just read it out loud here, uh,

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:39.740
"The formulation of a problem is often more essential than its solution,

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:43.330
which may be merely a matter of mathematical or experimental skill.

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:44.730
To raise new questions,

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:48.620
new problems to rega- regard old problems from a new angle required,

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:52.460
requires creative imagination and marks real advance in science".

00:10:52.460 --> 00:10:55.670
And I think that most people generally will believe this

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:59.125
to be true and I certainly have in time that I've been working on this.

00:10:59.125 --> 00:11:03.225
Um and what, what Einstein is really talking about here is what, is the,

00:11:03.225 --> 00:11:07.485
the language that we use of reframing problems.

00:11:07.485 --> 00:11:09.540
But the question, I think,

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:11.280
that most people run into is, yes,

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:12.930
I understand the value of that,

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:15.060
but how do I do that?

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:18.435
And that's what we spent the last 6-7 years, um,

00:11:18.435 --> 00:11:22.845
really thinking about and developing tools to help people do this.

00:11:22.845 --> 00:11:26.625
I think it's worth mentioning here that our approach to this,

00:11:26.625 --> 00:11:27.915
uh, is like I mentioned earlier,

00:11:27.915 --> 00:11:30.240
has been through the lens of educators and

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:33.315
practitioners of design, thinking and innovation.

00:11:33.315 --> 00:11:35.790
And in, in that practice, um,

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:39.885
our practice is to borrow tools and techniques from

00:11:39.885 --> 00:11:45.210
many different disciplines and to think about how we might use them in an applied way,

00:11:45.210 --> 00:11:48.570
that are designed to be as useful to,

00:11:48.570 --> 00:11:50.820
um, the people using them as possible.

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:55.830
And so our, our lens really is in making tools for people to use,

00:11:55.830 --> 00:11:58.455
and in this case, I'm going to show you some of those tools that we've used.

00:11:58.455 --> 00:12:00.090
I should also note that, um,

00:12:00.090 --> 00:12:04.115
we're not traditional academics and that we don't do research on this stuff.

00:12:04.115 --> 00:12:06.795
We often look at research and borrow, um,

00:12:06.795 --> 00:12:09.000
what we see as being tools that we can get for,

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:10.530
for people to use like you.

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:12.960
Uh, and I also say that, um,

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:16.380
there is a fair amount of literature out there around,

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:19.115
uh, problem definition, problem solving,

00:12:19.115 --> 00:12:22.230
and um, I'm guessing that some of

00:12:22.230 --> 00:12:25.380
this will be familiar to those of you who work in those fields,

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:26.585
and if I, um,

00:12:26.585 --> 00:12:28.050
haven't cited a reference to your work,

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:29.400
I would love to hear from you.

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:32.730
Uh, I would love to read what you've produced as a way to better,

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:35.550
uh, improve the, the, and inform things that we're doing.

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:41.730
Okay. So the, the one tool that I'll start with, um,

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:45.690
in demonstrating today, is this idea of reframing problems

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:50.445
by identifying causality and constraints around the problem.

00:12:50.445 --> 00:12:55.575
And the way that I'm gonna do this is by way of telling a, a, story,

00:12:55.575 --> 00:12:59.345
and the story comes from an article that was written in the Harvard Business Review,

00:12:59.345 --> 00:13:01.115
I think, uh, five or six years ago.

00:13:01.115 --> 00:13:03.390
Uh, it's cited at the end of these slides,

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:06.410
and I'll show this to you, where basically,

00:13:06.410 --> 00:13:11.580
the story is of a building manager,

00:13:11.580 --> 00:13:14.160
and uh, uh, the building, uh,

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:15.420
we were in a time in like in,

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:16.725
like the mid 1950s,

00:13:16.725 --> 00:13:21.765
when um, building tech- elevator technology was advancing rapidly,

00:13:21.765 --> 00:13:23.410
and so a lot of people, uh,

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:25.140
in the city were experiencing these,

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:29.535
these elevators that were much faster or becoming much faster,

00:13:29.535 --> 00:13:31.340
and as a result of that,

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:34.080
the occupants, the tenants in his building began to

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:38.460
complain about the speed of the elevator in their building.

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:43.560
And so originally, the building manager defined the problem as this one here.

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:48.990
The problem is that people are complaining about the speed of the elevator [NOISE].

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:52.545
And so originally, the building manager was thinking about this and

00:13:52.545 --> 00:13:56.595
immediately jumped to thinking about solutions to this problem,

00:13:56.595 --> 00:13:59.175
and as with most people,

00:13:59.175 --> 00:14:02.190
the first ones that come to mind are obvious solutions to this problem.

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:04.350
And a few that he came to mi- that came to mind for

00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:07.770
him were that maybe the elevator needed a faster motor,

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:12.960
maybe there was a new or better algorithm to make the elevator more efficient, um,

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:15.780
and another option, another potential solution was just to,

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:18.810
uh, put in a new elevator altogether.

00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:23.880
According the, the, the building manager really started to think about this [NOISE],

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:26.820
and the question he asked himself was,

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:30.255
what might be the, the causes of this problem?

00:14:30.255 --> 00:14:32.160
And so what he did is he created

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:35.730
a list on a piece of paper just like the one that you have in front of you,

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:39.300
and the potential causes that he identified for this were,

00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:40.865
uh, an obvious one here,

00:14:40.865 --> 00:14:44.055
um, the elevator maybe isn't working properly.

00:14:44.055 --> 00:14:48.090
Uh, he had noted that new elevators in other buildings were becoming faster,

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:51.150
so maybe that's a part of the problem of people complaining.

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:52.815
And then the third one here, uh,

00:14:52.815 --> 00:14:55.320
which I thought was pretty interesting is this id- this,

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:57.930
this potential cause that he identified were maybe it's that

00:14:57.930 --> 00:15:00.900
people don't have anything to do while they wait.

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:02.805
And so we're gonna come back to that one,

00:15:02.805 --> 00:15:05.190
but in thinking about the causes of the problem,

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:08.050
this is what he had come up with.

00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:11.070
So then he got to thinking about, well, okay.

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:13.230
If I'm gonna begin solving these problems,

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:16.460
certainly I'm gonna be working with some constraints.

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:19.560
And so the next thing that he did was he asked himself,

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:21.450
himself, you know, what are

00:15:21.450 --> 00:15:24.465
the constraints that I might encounter in solving this problem?

00:15:24.465 --> 00:15:26.355
And a couple that he came up with, uh,

00:15:26.355 --> 00:15:28.740
were those listed here which is, well,

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:30.945
we don't really have a budget for a new elevator,

00:15:30.945 --> 00:15:33.300
so we would need to get new funding to do that.

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:35.460
And in terms of making the elevator,

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:37.575
the existing elevator more efficient, um,

00:15:37.575 --> 00:15:40.875
there were not better algorithms on the market,

00:15:40.875 --> 00:15:42.630
uh, to help him do that.

00:15:42.630 --> 00:15:49.505
[NOISE] Uh, and I think he,

00:15:49.505 --> 00:15:52.680
what was really interesting about this example is that he

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:57.120
realized that he could turn all of these causes and all of these,

00:15:57.120 --> 00:16:00.705
uh, constraints into problems themselves,

00:16:00.705 --> 00:16:03.435
which you can see listed here.

00:16:03.435 --> 00:16:05.525
And as he was thinking about this,

00:16:05.525 --> 00:16:07.965
he had this sort of 'aha' moment,

00:16:07.965 --> 00:16:10.515
and the aha moment was that,

00:16:10.515 --> 00:16:13.800
I can choose which of these problems to solve.

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:17.040
And I think this is an important moment and it illustrates the plan made earlier,

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:18.630
which is that for the first time,

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:23.490
he realized that he had agency here to actually define the problem, right?

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:25.530
He started with this original problem that people are

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:27.665
complaining about the speed of the elevator,

00:16:27.665 --> 00:16:31.385
but by identifying the actual causes or potential causes of that problem,

00:16:31.385 --> 00:16:33.370
thinking through the constraints and trying to solve

00:16:33.370 --> 00:16:35.820
the problem and then reframing in these problems,

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:38.550
he had this moment of realizing that there are

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:43.155
more than one problem to be solved here and that he can choose which one to do so.

00:16:43.155 --> 00:16:44.940
But then the question was,

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:46.500
which one should I choose?

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:54.845
[NOISE] And as he was thinking about this, um,

00:16:54.845 --> 00:16:57.990
he was trying to think through all of the potential questions and things that

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:01.470
he would need to think about in deciding which one to choose,

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:03.885
and he came up with a, with a list of things.

00:17:03.885 --> 00:17:06.545
The first one was, um,

00:17:06.545 --> 00:17:10.490
for which of these problems do I have the right leverage or influence?

00:17:10.490 --> 00:17:15.280
Second one was, for which do I have the right abilities and competencies?

00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:16.440
Like, can I do this or not?

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:18.605
Do I have the knowledge or skills?

00:17:18.605 --> 00:17:23.340
The third one was, um, for which one do I have the right resources?

00:17:23.340 --> 00:17:25.305
Do I have enough money or time to,

00:17:25.305 --> 00:17:27.465
to be able to solve this problem?

00:17:27.465 --> 00:17:31.215
Another one was, if I try to solve a constraint in particular, uh,

00:17:31.215 --> 00:17:34.960
how do I make sure that I'm not just amplifying a cause of the problem to the extent that

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:38.625
it's not focusing in on what is perceived to be the actual cause of,

00:17:38.625 --> 00:17:42.455
of this problem [NOISE], of people complaining about the elevator being too slow?

00:17:42.455 --> 00:17:44.650
Then the last one was, uh,

00:17:44.650 --> 00:17:46.625
and I think this is a really critical one,

00:17:46.625 --> 00:17:50.910
what are the potential unintended consequences of solving each of these problems?

00:17:50.910 --> 00:17:54.340
[NOISE] And so he went through, uh,

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:56.930
the list of all the problems that he identified

00:17:56.930 --> 00:18:00.600
and was thinking through this list of questions that he had,

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:02.145
and so the first one was, yeah.

00:18:02.145 --> 00:18:06.080
'The, the problem was if the elevator isn't working properly,' and so um,

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:11.145
he immediately, uh, diagnosed the elevator and it turned out that it was working fine,

00:18:11.145 --> 00:18:13.055
so that was not the problem.

00:18:13.055 --> 00:18:15.815
He took a look at 'the problem is the elevator, uh,

00:18:15.815 --> 00:18:19.320
or that new elevators in other buildings are becoming much faster,'

00:18:19.320 --> 00:18:23.365
but he realized that he didn't really have any leverage or influence in changing that,

00:18:23.365 --> 00:18:25.030
and so that wasn't, apparently,

00:18:25.030 --> 00:18:27.200
a very good problem for him to tackle.

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:30.300
He took a look at 'the problem is that people don't have anything to do

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:32.735
while they wait,' and on this one, he,

00:18:32.735 --> 00:18:36.365
he thought to himself, "Well, I may or may not ha- have the right abilities or resources,

00:18:36.365 --> 00:18:38.430
but I'm not sure."

00:18:38.430 --> 00:18:42.270
He then took a look at 'the, the constraints as he had turned them into problems,

00:18:42.270 --> 00:18:44.615
and the problem is that we don't have a budget for a new

00:18:44.615 --> 00:18:47.270
elevator,' and as he thought about this,

00:18:47.270 --> 00:18:49.620
the question that came to mind was, well,

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:51.740
if we raise rents to buy a new elevator,

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:54.605
we might actually draw more attention to the elevator itself,

00:18:54.605 --> 00:18:56.130
and so it seemed to him that there might be

00:18:56.130 --> 00:19:00.230
an unattended consequence here of actually trying to fix the elevator.

00:19:00.230 --> 00:19:02.380
And in the last one, um,

00:19:02.380 --> 00:19:05.895
'the problem is that the better algorithms don't yet exist.'

00:19:05.895 --> 00:19:09.990
He thought to himself, "Well, I don't have the right abilities, uh, to do,

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:11.350
to design a better algorithm,

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:14.340
but if I did, I might just be creating this sort of,

00:19:14.340 --> 00:19:18.860
this idea of a reinforcing feedback loop of having to always have the fastest

00:19:18.860 --> 00:19:21.520
elevator," and so he would kinda get stuck in

00:19:21.520 --> 00:19:25.650
this trap of having to always be solving this problem.

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:30.270
And so as he stepped back and he took a look at all these problems that he identified,

00:19:30.270 --> 00:19:31.955
potential problems that he could begin, um,

00:19:31.955 --> 00:19:34.445
or that he could start with trying to solve,

00:19:34.445 --> 00:19:37.935
the one that he identified was this one,

00:19:37.935 --> 00:19:43.185
which is the problem, is that people don't have anything to do while they wait.

00:19:43.185 --> 00:19:47.325
And so um, he,

00:19:47.325 --> 00:19:49.710
as he was thinking about [NOISE] trying to solve this problem,

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:51.420
he did some research and,

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:57.000
um, what he discovered was that there's actually a lot of work done around,

00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:59.530
um, what's known as queuing theory [NOISE].

00:19:59.530 --> 00:20:03.225
And if you ever go to Disneyland or a large amusement park,

00:20:03.225 --> 00:20:05.525
they spend a lot of time thinking about how

00:20:05.525 --> 00:20:08.495
to entertain people while they're waiting for rides.

00:20:08.495 --> 00:20:12.855
Um, and what he discovered in that research was that,

00:20:12.855 --> 00:20:16.279
if you install mirrors around the elevator,

00:20:16.279 --> 00:20:17.925
as you can see here,

00:20:17.925 --> 00:20:22.050
people will be happy to wait if they have time to look at themselves,

00:20:22.050 --> 00:20:25.360
to prep themselves, and to look at how beautiful they are.

00:20:25.360 --> 00:20:27.720
And so I think that this is

00:20:27.720 --> 00:20:33.060
a really beautiful example of how reframing problems in this way,

00:20:33.060 --> 00:20:37.625
really thinking about, what are the real causes of the problem that I'm starting with?

00:20:37.625 --> 00:20:40.705
What are the constraints around trying to solve that problem?

00:20:40.705 --> 00:20:42.335
Thinking through those questions,

00:20:42.335 --> 00:20:44.030
around which ones are,

00:20:44.030 --> 00:20:46.535
um, do I have the abilities and competencies,

00:20:46.535 --> 00:20:49.455
the resources to solve,

00:20:49.455 --> 00:20:53.670
can lead to really easy elegant solutions.

00:20:53.670 --> 00:20:54.900
And the point of all this,

00:20:54.900 --> 00:21:00.210
the underlying reason why this works in terms of innovation is this;

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:05.825
reframing problems helps us get away from the obvious solutions, right?

00:21:05.825 --> 00:21:08.800
So had he, uh, only focused on

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:13.394
the original problem of people complaining about the speed of the elevator,

00:21:13.394 --> 00:21:17.075
he would have likely invested in a new motor,

00:21:17.075 --> 00:21:19.069
maybe a new control algorithm,

00:21:19.069 --> 00:21:22.020
or maybe he would have purchased a new elevator altogether.

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:25.125
But by reframing that original problem,

00:21:25.125 --> 00:21:29.404
what he was able to do was to define a new problem,

00:21:29.404 --> 00:21:33.015
and that led to less obvious solutions,

00:21:33.015 --> 00:21:36.630
uh, which for him were mirrors around doors.

00:21:36.630 --> 00:21:38.190
And what you see here is,

00:21:38.190 --> 00:21:40.320
is a representa- representation of

00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:44.625
the potential solution set that's related to how we define a problem,

00:21:44.625 --> 00:21:45.945
and in this case,

00:21:45.945 --> 00:21:47.890
by re- reframing the problem,

00:21:47.890 --> 00:21:52.565
he was able to open up the number of potential solutions, and when you do that,

00:21:52.565 --> 00:21:54.620
it becomes just a, a, a math, uh,

00:21:54.620 --> 00:21:56.160
equation and that you have

00:21:56.160 --> 00:22:00.440
many more potential solutions to draw from in solving the problem.

00:22:00.440 --> 00:22:03.750
This is really critical to how we think about, um,

00:22:03.750 --> 00:22:06.520
leading to or binding creative solutions,

00:22:06.520 --> 00:22:09.090
innovative solutions to existing problems.

00:22:09.090 --> 00:22:11.920
[NOISE] And so what I want you to do now is to

00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:14.940
take a look at the problem that you've identified or to come back to it in,

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:16.055
in your head if you, if you think,

00:22:16.055 --> 00:22:19.030
if you thought about it originally [NOISE],

00:22:19.030 --> 00:22:21.160
and as you're thinking about this problem,

00:22:21.160 --> 00:22:25.100
what I want you to realize is that this problem and

00:22:25.100 --> 00:22:30.260
every problem is caused by something else [NOISE].

00:22:30.260 --> 00:22:34.210
This problem and every problem is constrained by something,

00:22:35.070 --> 00:22:41.280
and importantly, these causes and constraints can be reframed as other problems.

00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:44.610
And as, as you're thinking about your problem,

00:22:44.610 --> 00:22:45.640
I would encourage you,

00:22:45.640 --> 00:22:46.875
either now or afterwards,

00:22:46.875 --> 00:22:51.295
to think about how you might do this [NOISE].

00:22:51.295 --> 00:22:55.075
I think it's really important to remember that

00:22:55.075 --> 00:22:58.510
re-framing problems often leads to unexpected and innovative solutions.

00:22:58.510 --> 00:23:01.780
I think that the mirrors around the elevator are a great example of this.

00:23:01.780 --> 00:23:02.950
But in the work that we've done,

00:23:02.950 --> 00:23:05.860
we've seen hundreds of examples of this happening.

00:23:05.860 --> 00:23:08.815
In fact, we see it everyday in the classes that we teach here.

00:23:08.815 --> 00:23:12.175
And I think it's important as you're doing this to be aware

00:23:12.175 --> 00:23:15.505
of unintended consequences when solving for constraints.

00:23:15.505 --> 00:23:20.350
Um, I'm going to talk a little bit about this later on but often,

00:23:20.350 --> 00:23:22.600
when we try to solve for constraints,

00:23:22.600 --> 00:23:28.195
we're either solving for time and money or for new technologies.

00:23:28.195 --> 00:23:32.035
And the challenge of solving for time and money is that, um,

00:23:32.035 --> 00:23:37.060
from our experience it often really limits the potential solution

00:23:37.060 --> 00:23:42.565
set and so it doesn't allow you to get to really creative innovative solutions.

00:23:42.565 --> 00:23:47.350
And a challenge with using new technology to solve constraints, which in,

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:48.970
in my experience of working with a lot of

00:23:48.970 --> 00:23:52.285
organizations is often the direction that they head,

00:23:52.285 --> 00:23:57.970
is that it's really difficult when solving for a constraint to, um,

00:23:57.970 --> 00:24:00.310
be unaware or not think about

00:24:00.310 --> 00:24:05.125
the potential unintended consequences that might result from that technology.

00:24:05.125 --> 00:24:07.840
We see this all the time where new technologies simply

00:24:07.840 --> 00:24:11.110
amplify the underlying cause of the problem that we're trying to solve.

00:24:11.110 --> 00:24:14.150
And I'm going use an example later to make that point.

00:24:14.610 --> 00:24:16.765
And then the last thing,

00:24:16.765 --> 00:24:19.030
as you're thinking about, um,

00:24:19.030 --> 00:24:22.015
this problem that you have, is the,

00:24:22.015 --> 00:24:23.320
the second aspect of,

00:24:23.320 --> 00:24:25.345
of what I want to share with you today.

00:24:25.345 --> 00:24:28.420
And it's this idea of always being mindful of

00:24:28.420 --> 00:24:32.860
human needs as we're thinking about re-framing and defining problems.

00:24:32.860 --> 00:24:35.410
And I'm going to spend a little time, um,

00:24:35.410 --> 00:24:40.975
talking about that now and I think that this is probably the best image

00:24:40.975 --> 00:24:46.735
that I have found to describe what I see hundreds of times with organizations.

00:24:46.735 --> 00:24:49.345
And this is true of

00:24:49.345 --> 00:24:53.050
entrepreneurial startups to established industry players

00:24:53.050 --> 00:24:54.865
that have been around for a long time.

00:24:54.865 --> 00:25:00.760
And the, the reality that I see is that most organizations tend to

00:25:00.760 --> 00:25:03.460
focus on the needs of the organizations and not

00:25:03.460 --> 00:25:06.250
squarely or specifically on the needs of their customers,

00:25:06.250 --> 00:25:08.800
clients, their service recipients,

00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:11.485
um, their students in some cases.

00:25:11.485 --> 00:25:14.365
And so the goal of defining problems is to make

00:25:14.365 --> 00:25:18.850
sure or to be aware of the fact that this exists pretty frequently,

00:25:18.850 --> 00:25:23.860
and to align these two things in ways that solve both needs.

00:25:23.860 --> 00:25:27.085
And so I want to run through an example of,

00:25:27.085 --> 00:25:29.050
uh, what this looks like and how to do this.

00:25:29.050 --> 00:25:34.255
So, um, I have a question for you and as you're looking at this picture,

00:25:34.255 --> 00:25:38.485
the question is what does this girl need?

00:25:38.485 --> 00:25:41.080
And I want you to just think about that for a couple seconds.

00:25:41.080 --> 00:25:48.550
[NOISE] That if you're

00:25:48.550 --> 00:25:52.195
like most of the students in our classes and most of the executives,

00:25:52.195 --> 00:25:54.475
uh, at the business school where I teach,

00:25:54.475 --> 00:25:57.070
the first couple of things that come to mind is that she needs

00:25:57.070 --> 00:26:00.655
a book or that she needs a ladder.

00:26:00.655 --> 00:26:04.315
And the question that I- that I want to ask of you is,

00:26:04.315 --> 00:26:07.970
why does she need a book and why does she need a ladder?

00:26:09.840 --> 00:26:17.380
And most often the answer to why does she need a book is she needs a way to learn.

00:26:17.380 --> 00:26:23.170
I think this is important, this is what we referred to as a needs ladder and well,

00:26:23.170 --> 00:26:26.725
in design as we think about needs,

00:26:26.725 --> 00:26:33.700
our goal is to identify both the explicit and implicit needs that people have.

00:26:33.700 --> 00:26:36.505
And so in this case, the explicit need would be,

00:26:36.505 --> 00:26:38.200
uh, a book or a ladder.

00:26:38.200 --> 00:26:42.400
Whereas the implicit need would be that she needs a way to learn.

00:26:42.400 --> 00:26:45.670
And this is really important um, again,

00:26:45.670 --> 00:26:48.730
in terms of defining problems to

00:26:48.730 --> 00:26:52.435
maximize the potential for innovative and creative solutions,

00:26:52.435 --> 00:26:54.745
and I want to show you again why that is.

00:26:54.745 --> 00:26:57.115
So in this example,

00:26:57.115 --> 00:26:59.740
the obvious or explicit need that

00:26:59.740 --> 00:27:03.805
most people identify in that example is that she needs a ladder or a book.

00:27:03.805 --> 00:27:06.175
But if we open up the question, uh,

00:27:06.175 --> 00:27:09.145
to her need as being a way to learn,

00:27:09.145 --> 00:27:13.615
what that does is it opens up the total potential solution set, right?

00:27:13.615 --> 00:27:17.470
There are many potential solutions to a way to learn for her.

00:27:17.470 --> 00:27:20.545
Whereas a ladder or a book are very specific solutions.

00:27:20.545 --> 00:27:26.140
And again, this is an example of how by identifying these implicit needs,

00:27:26.140 --> 00:27:28.990
we're able to just open up the potential solution set.

00:27:28.990 --> 00:27:32.560
And what that does is it offers an opportunity to identify

00:27:32.560 --> 00:27:37.940
truly innovative and new creative ways of solving problems for people.

00:27:37.940 --> 00:27:41.790
Now, there's another aspect of this is important to keep in mind and

00:27:41.790 --> 00:27:44.955
this is a picture of Maslow's Hierarchy,

00:27:44.955 --> 00:27:47.345
uh, with which some of you are probably familiar.

00:27:47.345 --> 00:27:53.170
Um, I think those of you who are familiar with it know that in psychology

00:27:53.170 --> 00:27:58.915
this has become a defunct way of thinking about psychology and people.

00:27:58.915 --> 00:28:02.020
But for our purposes is actually really useful,

00:28:02.020 --> 00:28:07.840
and the reason that it's useful is that it is a pretty fair representation of,

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:11.800
um, moving from explicit needs,

00:28:11.800 --> 00:28:16.515
which in this case, are more like physiological needs around food, breathing, water.

00:28:16.515 --> 00:28:22.350
Towards moving up to more implicit needs that people have around social,

00:28:22.350 --> 00:28:24.980
psychological, and emotional needs.

00:28:24.980 --> 00:28:27.940
And the value of this is that what we know

00:28:27.940 --> 00:28:31.720
from a lot of experience and from a lot of demonstration,

00:28:31.720 --> 00:28:34.570
is that if you're able to frame a problem that

00:28:34.570 --> 00:28:37.810
incorporates an implicit, a social, emotional,

00:28:37.810 --> 00:28:43.420
or psychological need of a person or of people and to design a solution for that need,

00:28:43.420 --> 00:28:49.210
you're far more likely to design something that that person will actually want to use.

00:28:49.210 --> 00:28:51.115
And as we think about, um,

00:28:51.115 --> 00:28:53.350
innovation, getting new people to try new things,

00:28:53.350 --> 00:29:00.085
this a really critical aspect of driving innovation in, through problem definition.

00:29:00.085 --> 00:29:07.690
Okay. So I want to come back to the example of the building manager and

00:29:07.690 --> 00:29:11.020
the problems that he identified having

00:29:11.020 --> 00:29:15.265
moved through identifying the causes and the constraints and turning those into problems.

00:29:15.265 --> 00:29:22.825
Um, what- what you'll note here is that the one that he chose focused on people.

00:29:22.825 --> 00:29:25.690
Now, this is coincidental to a certain extent,

00:29:25.690 --> 00:29:27.520
but I think it's a great way of

00:29:27.520 --> 00:29:31.315
illustrating the example or the point that I'm making here.

00:29:31.315 --> 00:29:37.940
And so what I want you to do now is to take a look at the problem that you've identified.

00:29:38.430 --> 00:29:41.665
And what I'd like you to do is to ask yourself,

00:29:41.665 --> 00:29:44.575
if you haven't already involved people in some way in,

00:29:44.575 --> 00:29:45.790
in the definition of the problem,

00:29:45.790 --> 00:29:50.480
is how could you re-frame this problem to involve people in some way?

00:29:50.490 --> 00:29:54.235
How could you identify an explicit need here?

00:29:54.235 --> 00:29:56.995
Which is to say, what do these people need to be able to

00:29:56.995 --> 00:30:00.910
do as part of the definition of your problem?

00:30:00.910 --> 00:30:04.210
And then the last one and this is really critical,

00:30:04.210 --> 00:30:07.615
is as you're thinking about defining or re-framing this problem,

00:30:07.615 --> 00:30:11.200
how can you re-frame the problem in a way that identifies a need,

00:30:11.200 --> 00:30:14.875
uh, for the people in this problem to feel?

00:30:14.875 --> 00:30:20.410
And I think that in my experience of working with students and executives,

00:30:20.410 --> 00:30:25.930
that this is probably the most critical aspect of thinking about how to define problems.

00:30:25.930 --> 00:30:28.870
Um, if we can't or aren't able to identify

00:30:28.870 --> 00:30:31.900
and solve for needs that people have in this regard,

00:30:31.900 --> 00:30:37.360
it's very unlikely that the solutions we're going to develop will be adopted or,

00:30:37.360 --> 00:30:41.200
um, desired by the people for whom we're designing [NOISE].

00:30:41.200 --> 00:30:47.320
[NOISE] But I- I wanted to leave with kind of the story of the work that we've

00:30:47.320 --> 00:30:50.740
been doing as a way to illustrate I think what I

00:30:50.740 --> 00:30:54.160
have come to see as the most important thing around problem definition.

00:30:54.160 --> 00:30:58.650
And so I mentioned earlier this tension between, um,

00:30:58.650 --> 00:31:00.720
this problem that our agriculture is trying to solve,

00:31:00.720 --> 00:31:02.340
which is the problem is that farmers need to meet

00:31:02.340 --> 00:31:05.070
the nutritional demands of a growing population.

00:31:05.070 --> 00:31:07.320
Yet, at the same time, uh,

00:31:07.320 --> 00:31:11.400
we're seeing, uh, that our farmland end up looking like this.

00:31:11.400 --> 00:31:13.895
We're effectively destroying the soil.

00:31:13.895 --> 00:31:16.645
And I want to tell a story,

00:31:16.645 --> 00:31:19.015
a personal story to help illustrate,

00:31:19.015 --> 00:31:21.400
I think, what is at the fundamental challenge of this now that-

00:31:21.400 --> 00:31:24.385
that I think exists within many industries and sectors.

00:31:24.385 --> 00:31:27.595
So this is a picture of me and my grandpa when I was young.

00:31:27.595 --> 00:31:30.100
And my grandpa was, uh,

00:31:30.100 --> 00:31:31.510
fond of telling this story,

00:31:31.510 --> 00:31:32.830
although I never- I never heard it.

00:31:32.830 --> 00:31:34.945
My dad actually told it to me when- when,

00:31:34.945 --> 00:31:36.775
um, when he was sick.

00:31:36.775 --> 00:31:40.180
And the story is how the tractor ruined farming.

00:31:40.180 --> 00:31:45.070
And the story is- basically, it goes like this.

00:31:45.070 --> 00:31:48.925
Originally, the- the primary technology on our farms were horses,

00:31:48.925 --> 00:31:50.710
and- and that was fine.

00:31:50.710 --> 00:31:52.000
Um, we get up in the morning,

00:31:52.000 --> 00:31:53.650
we feed the horses, we feed ourselves,

00:31:53.650 --> 00:31:55.615
we saddle them up, we take them out,

00:31:55.615 --> 00:31:56.650
we'd work until noon,

00:31:56.650 --> 00:31:58.869
we'd come back, we'd eat, uh,

00:31:58.869 --> 00:32:01.330
and then we'd go back and we work in the afternoon until either we

00:32:01.330 --> 00:32:04.510
couldn't work anymore or the horses couldn't work anymore.

00:32:04.510 --> 00:32:07.045
And then one day, um,

00:32:07.045 --> 00:32:08.740
the tractor was invented.

00:32:08.740 --> 00:32:13.900
And th- the promise of the tractor was amazing.

00:32:13.900 --> 00:32:16.480
It was gonna make a lot of money for farmers,

00:32:16.480 --> 00:32:18.310
it was gonna save us a lot of time,

00:32:18.310 --> 00:32:20.350
um, we were gonna be happier.

00:32:20.350 --> 00:32:24.280
And so we bought a tractor, and everything was great.

00:32:24.280 --> 00:32:26.875
Uh, the tractor was definitely more efficient.

00:32:26.875 --> 00:32:29.875
But we didn't realize a couple of things in buying the tractor.

00:32:29.875 --> 00:32:33.519
And one was that this tractor required maintenance,

00:32:33.519 --> 00:32:35.965
uh, it required fuel.

00:32:35.965 --> 00:32:41.095
And as farmers, we didn't have a ton of money on the balance sheet.

00:32:41.095 --> 00:32:42.820
And so we had to borrow money,

00:32:42.820 --> 00:32:46.255
we had to take out an operating loan to- to- just to run the tractor.

00:32:46.255 --> 00:32:48.340
And that was on top of the loan we had to sneak out to buy

00:32:48.340 --> 00:32:50.995
the tractor. And so that was fine.

00:32:50.995 --> 00:32:53.365
But what we realized was that,

00:32:53.365 --> 00:32:56.560
this tractor was actually so productive that we

00:32:56.560 --> 00:33:00.355
didn't have enough farmland to actually make it pay for itself.

00:33:00.355 --> 00:33:05.800
And so what we had to do was we had to buy more land just to make the tractor,

00:33:05.800 --> 00:33:07.240
um, pay for itself.

00:33:07.240 --> 00:33:08.800
And so as a result of that,

00:33:08.800 --> 00:33:10.525
we took out another mortgage,

00:33:10.525 --> 00:33:12.160
we bought more land,

00:33:12.160 --> 00:33:15.040
and we started farm- farming efficiently,

00:33:15.040 --> 00:33:17.290
and finally, we were able to pay off the tractor.

00:33:17.290 --> 00:33:19.660
And for a short period of time, this was great.

00:33:19.660 --> 00:33:21.370
We were making a little bit more money,

00:33:21.370 --> 00:33:23.125
we were definitely more efficient.

00:33:23.125 --> 00:33:28.195
But what happened was that everybody started buying tractors.

00:33:28.195 --> 00:33:30.805
And everybody was buying more land,

00:33:30.805 --> 00:33:33.055
and everybody was producing more food.

00:33:33.055 --> 00:33:35.365
And in the short period of time,

00:33:35.365 --> 00:33:36.910
as a result of that,

00:33:36.910 --> 00:33:38.680
was, uh, more food on the market.

00:33:38.680 --> 00:33:41.440
The price of that food began to decline.

00:33:41.440 --> 00:33:43.315
For consumers, this was great.

00:33:43.315 --> 00:33:45.820
But for farmers, this wasn't so great.

00:33:45.820 --> 00:33:51.355
What it meant was that from moving from the horses to tractors,

00:33:51.355 --> 00:33:53.530
we had taken on a bunch of debt.

00:33:53.530 --> 00:33:56.050
And at the end of the day, because a falling through prices,

00:33:56.050 --> 00:33:59.005
we weren't any better off economically.

00:33:59.005 --> 00:34:02.154
And as my dad was telling me this story,

00:34:02.154 --> 00:34:06.340
I realized that this was an allegory for

00:34:06.340 --> 00:34:12.040
pretty much every technology that has been developed in our agriculture since.

00:34:12.040 --> 00:34:16.270
And it's all been in service of this question of how are we

00:34:16.270 --> 00:34:20.185
going to feed all of these people in this growing population?

00:34:20.185 --> 00:34:25.555
And so when I saw our farm looking like this,

00:34:25.555 --> 00:34:30.610
that was the moment when I realized I think that we're solving the wrong problem here.

00:34:30.610 --> 00:34:35.290
And when you think about what is the real problem that we're trying to solve,

00:34:35.290 --> 00:34:38.500
I was notified of this- this fact a couple of years ago,

00:34:38.500 --> 00:34:40.480
and it really struck me.

00:34:40.480 --> 00:34:42.325
And I've reframed it here.

00:34:42.325 --> 00:34:46.330
The problem is that the United Nations Food and Agri Organization estimates that

00:34:46.330 --> 00:34:50.590
we have 60 global harvests remaining before we're unable to feed everyone.

00:34:50.590 --> 00:34:54.025
And this is largely a result of the way that we farm,

00:34:54.025 --> 00:34:59.270
and by literally destroying the soil on which we rely to grow food.

00:35:02.370 --> 00:35:05.620
It's clear to me, through my experience that

00:35:05.620 --> 00:35:08.575
the problem we're trying to solve is the wrong problem.

00:35:08.575 --> 00:35:13.375
And I think what I've learned is that the solution to this,

00:35:13.375 --> 00:35:15.760
and generally speaking, this is true of this problem,

00:35:15.760 --> 00:35:18.025
and it's true of the problem you have in front of you.

00:35:18.025 --> 00:35:21.400
Is that the solution is to solve the causes of these problems,

00:35:21.400 --> 00:35:24.235
and to solve the implicit needs of people.

00:35:24.235 --> 00:35:28.390
And I'm confident based on all of our experience in the work that we're doing,

00:35:28.390 --> 00:35:30.535
and through many examples of, uh,

00:35:30.535 --> 00:35:33.505
thought leaders and others in the food system who are doing this.

00:35:33.505 --> 00:35:37.630
That if we begin to think this way and frame our pro- problems in this way,

00:35:37.630 --> 00:35:39.970
then we truly have an opportunity to solve some

00:35:39.970 --> 00:35:42.250
of these deeply systemic issues that we face,

00:35:42.250 --> 00:35:45.160
and whatever the sectors and industries are that we operate.

00:35:45.160 --> 00:35:47.320
And I'm hoping that by way of today,

00:35:47.320 --> 00:35:51.610
that this gives you some tools for you to do this within your organizations,

00:35:51.610 --> 00:35:55.930
and it gives you some power to reframe these problems in ways

00:35:55.930 --> 00:36:00.865
that will result in positive outcomes for people and our planet.

00:36:00.865 --> 00:36:05.905
And I think that that's all that I've got for today. Thank you.

00:36:05.905 --> 00:36:08.020
Great. Thank you very much,

00:36:08.020 --> 00:36:10.210
Matt for this very informative presentation.

00:36:10.210 --> 00:36:12.655
And I hope that all of you have spent some time,

00:36:12.655 --> 00:36:15.310
rethinking the organizational challenge you wrote

00:36:15.310 --> 00:36:19.310
down in the very beginning of Matt's framework and ideas.

00:36:19.320 --> 00:36:23.335
Okay. So I am seeing that we have,

00:36:23.335 --> 00:36:25.780
uh, few questions along the same theme.

00:36:25.780 --> 00:36:28.030
So I will make sure to,

00:36:28.030 --> 00:36:29.215
uh, propose that one first.

00:36:29.215 --> 00:36:33.325
So people are asking about, um,

00:36:33.325 --> 00:36:34.840
you know, finding, you know,

00:36:34.840 --> 00:36:38.755
talking about finding the problem, identifying the problem,

00:36:38.755 --> 00:36:45.865
um, and so to sum up several of these different types of questions,

00:36:45.865 --> 00:36:48.400
people would first like to know,

00:36:48.400 --> 00:36:53.500
um, what kind of preparation or legwork should

00:36:53.500 --> 00:36:58.630
they be doing before they feel like they're ready to identify the real problem?

00:36:58.630 --> 00:37:02.005
Is there any- anything that they should- should do?

00:37:02.005 --> 00:37:04.540
Yeah. No. I- I think, um, you know, uh,

00:37:04.540 --> 00:37:07.495
as we've thought about these tools, um,

00:37:07.495 --> 00:37:11.755
I think the real value of them is that they can be applied today,

00:37:11.755 --> 00:37:13.900
to whatever the problem is that you're working on.

00:37:13.900 --> 00:37:17.770
And this could be- we've done this with teams and with executives,

00:37:17.770 --> 00:37:22.180
um, who- who encounter problems on a daily basis, uh, recurring problems.

00:37:22.180 --> 00:37:23.980
It could be in the strategic setting.

00:37:23.980 --> 00:37:26.890
So it is really designed as a way to, uh,

00:37:26.890 --> 00:37:29.725
think about whatever the problem is that's in front of you.

00:37:29.725 --> 00:37:32.680
Whether it's something that's happening that you didn't expect,

00:37:32.680 --> 00:37:36.310
or whether there's something that you're thinking about long-term or strategically.

00:37:36.310 --> 00:37:39.160
Um, and so- so really,

00:37:39.160 --> 00:37:42.685
the preparation is just wherever you're at, begin there.

00:37:42.685 --> 00:37:49.120
Great. And, uh, would you say- so this is the other side of the question that's gotten.

00:37:49.120 --> 00:37:52.525
Um, would you say that's the same if you're trying to,

00:37:52.525 --> 00:37:56.410
um, find kind of the cause of the consumer problem?

00:37:56.410 --> 00:37:58.570
So if you look at it the other way,

00:37:58.570 --> 00:38:02.185
not internal, but the external, identifying their problems.

00:38:02.185 --> 00:38:04.540
Yeah. For sure.

00:38:04.540 --> 00:38:07.540
Um, and I think- so there- I guess there are a couple of things,

00:38:07.540 --> 00:38:08.830
uh, ar- around that.

00:38:08.830 --> 00:38:14.575
One is, um, we- and this gets into,

00:38:14.575 --> 00:38:18.295
uh, another aspect of- of design thinking today- I haven't touched on today.

00:38:18.295 --> 00:38:22.120
Um, the design thinking is also known as human-centered design.

00:38:22.120 --> 00:38:24.160
Um, and what that really means is that,

00:38:24.160 --> 00:38:25.945
if you're thinking about, um,

00:38:25.945 --> 00:38:28.030
how to design something, whether it's a product,

00:38:28.030 --> 00:38:30.910
or a service, or experience for a consumer, or a customer,

00:38:30.910 --> 00:38:34.285
or client, or somebody external to the organization, um,

00:38:34.285 --> 00:38:38.050
we- we often believe that we both know what their problem is,

00:38:38.050 --> 00:38:39.835
and what the solution is to that.

00:38:39.835 --> 00:38:43.480
And as a fundamental assumption that needs to be challenged.

00:38:43.480 --> 00:38:43.960
Mm-hmm.

00:38:43.960 --> 00:38:46.270
And the way to challenge that assumption is by actually

00:38:46.270 --> 00:38:48.790
talking to your customers, your clients,

00:38:48.790 --> 00:38:51.280
and your- and your customers to identify what are

00:38:51.280 --> 00:38:55.480
the real both explicit and implicit needs that they have,

00:38:55.480 --> 00:38:59.320
and then determine that learning into what you think that the problem is.

00:38:59.320 --> 00:39:02.020
Now, I will say, and I think it's really critical,

00:39:02.020 --> 00:39:04.630
in- in every aspect of thinking about problems,

00:39:04.630 --> 00:39:08.920
is that, um, when we define a problem,

00:39:08.920 --> 00:39:12.310
I think the best way to think about it is as a hypothesis and not as-

00:39:12.310 --> 00:39:12.490
Mm-hmm.

00:39:12.490 --> 00:39:14.545
-um, the, you know,

00:39:14.545 --> 00:39:16.810
set in stone statement of what we think the problem is.

00:39:16.810 --> 00:39:21.445
You know, we are inferring what we think the problem is.

00:39:21.445 --> 00:39:25.540
And in design what we teach is to think about that problem as a hypothesis to

00:39:25.540 --> 00:39:27.910
develop some ideas or concepts that we think

00:39:27.910 --> 00:39:30.415
might solve the need or problem that we've identified.

00:39:30.415 --> 00:39:34.090
And to test that a bunch of times is a way to both refine

00:39:34.090 --> 00:39:36.070
the framing of the problem based on what we're learning

00:39:36.070 --> 00:39:38.620
through th- through the testing and the experimentation,

00:39:38.620 --> 00:39:43.795
as well as to understand is- is this idea solving the need that we've identified or not.

00:39:43.795 --> 00:39:46.900
And I think that there's a lot of value to doing that.

00:39:46.900 --> 00:39:49.060
Um, first of all, it ensures that the problem that you're

00:39:49.060 --> 00:39:51.460
focusing on is a real problem to be solved,

00:39:51.460 --> 00:39:53.500
that people have these real needs.

00:39:53.500 --> 00:39:56.200
And it also saves a lot of time and money, um,

00:39:56.200 --> 00:39:58.585
before investing a lot of money

00:39:58.585 --> 00:40:01.690
into what we think of as being the solution to your problem,

00:40:01.690 --> 00:40:06.010
only to realize once it's been out in the market that nobody actually wants it.

00:40:06.010 --> 00:40:09.340
And, um, we refer to this, uh,

00:40:09.340 --> 00:40:12.970
pretty frequently as technologies in sear- in- in- in,

00:40:12.970 --> 00:40:14.860
um, search of a need.

00:40:14.860 --> 00:40:18.025
And we see this all the time here in Silicon Valley,

00:40:18.025 --> 00:40:22.370
um, that I- I've been a part of a startup for which that was the case as well.

00:40:22.370 --> 00:40:30.300
So, uh, several people are also asking, um,

00:40:30.300 --> 00:40:34.199
so once a problem has been identified and properly framed,

00:40:34.199 --> 00:40:39.090
is there a certain thought process in identifying the steps towards the solution?

00:40:39.090 --> 00:40:42.910
Yeah. Um, so once you have a problem in mind,

00:40:42.910 --> 00:40:46.840
again, I think the most important thing is to think about it as a hypothesis.

00:40:46.840 --> 00:40:50.740
And, um, in terms of moving towards solutions,

00:40:50.740 --> 00:40:54.640
uh, what we recommend is, uh, and what we practice,

00:40:54.640 --> 00:40:57.415
and this is- this is a core to design thinking, um,

00:40:57.415 --> 00:41:02.125
is to develop a number of potential solutions to that problem.

00:41:02.125 --> 00:41:03.520
Right? And so, you know,

00:41:03.520 --> 00:41:06.475
the- the relationship between problem definition,

00:41:06.475 --> 00:41:09.970
as I mentioned earlier, and a potential solution set is really important.

00:41:09.970 --> 00:41:11.350
So you've got the right framing,

00:41:11.350 --> 00:41:13.000
it's going to open up the potential solutions.

00:41:13.000 --> 00:41:16.885
And what that really means is that there are a lot of potential solutions in that.

00:41:16.885 --> 00:41:20.740
The goal is to identify as many of those as possible,

00:41:20.740 --> 00:41:23.495
and then degrade a way to think about which of them,

00:41:23.495 --> 00:41:27.600
um, is likely to solve the- the need or a problem that we have.

00:41:27.600 --> 00:41:30.945
Which do we have the resources available to pursue?

00:41:30.945 --> 00:41:33.795
Which of them are really creative and innovative?

00:41:33.795 --> 00:41:36.155
Which of them, um,

00:41:36.155 --> 00:41:39.055
might or might not create unintended consequence,

00:41:39.055 --> 00:41:40.795
if we're thinking about,

00:41:40.795 --> 00:41:43.490
uh, bringing them to life? Um.

00:41:43.770 --> 00:41:46.480
Then, to before, again,

00:41:46.480 --> 00:41:48.745
before taking those ideas from that,

00:41:48.745 --> 00:41:50.875
from that potential set,

00:41:50.875 --> 00:41:53.770
to do some very early testing of those ideas,

00:41:53.770 --> 00:41:56.230
and we literally do notes on pieces of paper in front

00:41:56.230 --> 00:41:58.900
of the people for whom we're solving just to get a sense of,

00:41:58.900 --> 00:42:00.550
is this idea great or not?

00:42:00.550 --> 00:42:01.900
Is this really the need that you've

00:42:01.900 --> 00:42:04.195
identified with the problem that we're trying to solve?

00:42:04.195 --> 00:42:06.070
And then doing that, many times,

00:42:06.070 --> 00:42:09.910
in an innovative way to get to a place where we feel like that idea or

00:42:09.910 --> 00:42:11.860
solution concept is really leading

00:42:11.860 --> 00:42:14.545
the meter in solving the problem that we've identified.

00:42:14.545 --> 00:42:20.065
Great. Um, so people are also asking a lot of questions about,

00:42:20.065 --> 00:42:23.500
um, hidden and implicit needs and identifying those.

00:42:23.500 --> 00:42:23.860
Yeah.

00:42:23.860 --> 00:42:29.950
So, um, do you have any techniques for really discovering these?

00:42:29.950 --> 00:42:35.440
Especially, if it's a society that perhaps has poor communication, um,

00:42:35.440 --> 00:42:40.450
or you don't have the same type of accesses,

00:42:40.450 --> 00:42:45.010
you know, some of the companies in Silicon Valley may have to, to people at serving.

00:42:45.010 --> 00:42:48.910
Yeah. Um, so, what- what I'll say is that,

00:42:48.910 --> 00:42:51.910
generally speaking, um, there are a lot.

00:42:51.910 --> 00:42:54.010
In a corporate context, there are a lot,

00:42:54.010 --> 00:42:59.140
there are many constraints around using the techniques that we use in design to uncover,

00:42:59.140 --> 00:43:02.365
uh, the implicit needs that people have.

00:43:02.365 --> 00:43:06.580
Um, that- that would be hours of conversation to explain those,

00:43:06.580 --> 00:43:14.755
but in our context the goal of ide- how to identify the implicit needs that people have,

00:43:14.755 --> 00:43:22.690
is really under, trying to understand what they care about and what motivates them.

00:43:22.690 --> 00:43:26.020
The simplest way to put that is to

00:43:26.020 --> 00:43:30.505
understand why people say what they say in response to your,

00:43:30.505 --> 00:43:32.380
to the questions that you might ask.

00:43:32.380 --> 00:43:37.780
There is an expression of five whys to cry and medics put

00:43:37.780 --> 00:43:45.940
that expression as rooted in this idea that if you ask somebody why to the same question,

00:43:45.940 --> 00:43:48.460
same question and answer, um,

00:43:48.460 --> 00:43:54.290
you will eventually get to this thing that they most deeply care about.

00:43:54.290 --> 00:43:57.240
The goal in having conversations with

00:43:57.240 --> 00:44:00.480
our customers or clients or service recipients and our students,

00:44:00.480 --> 00:44:02.685
is really to understand at that level like,

00:44:02.685 --> 00:44:04.535
what do they really care about,

00:44:04.535 --> 00:44:06.850
and that helps to provide insight into

00:44:06.850 --> 00:44:09.640
the context of whatever the problem is that you're trying to solve.

00:44:09.640 --> 00:44:13.250
Whether it's as a product or a service or some kind of an experience.

00:44:13.740 --> 00:44:17.890
Great. So, I think we have time for maybe one or two more.

00:44:17.890 --> 00:44:21.505
So, people are asking about root cause analysis.

00:44:21.505 --> 00:44:21.790
