00:44:21.790 --> 00:44:24.790
Um, specifically, if it differs from

00:44:24.790 --> 00:44:28.390
problem identification or are they essentially the same.

00:44:28.390 --> 00:44:29.710
You know, so, um,

00:44:29.710 --> 00:44:31.540
I'll, I'll answer that in two ways.

00:44:31.540 --> 00:44:32.725
One is that, um,

00:44:32.725 --> 00:44:34.450
as I mentioned, I'm not an academic.

00:44:34.450 --> 00:44:39.970
So, um, if we pull the end and we also pull from a lot of different disciplines,

00:44:39.970 --> 00:44:43.960
um, I'm familiar with root cause analysis but I'm not an expert in it.

00:44:43.960 --> 00:44:46.120
But as I know it it's effectively into

00:44:46.120 --> 00:44:48.760
my mind essentially the same thing of really understanding,

00:44:48.760 --> 00:44:52.465
identifying what is the root cause of the problem that we're working on.

00:44:52.465 --> 00:44:54.700
I know that there are a lot of tools and techniques and

00:44:54.700 --> 00:44:57.415
processes that people use in that discipline to do that,

00:44:57.415 --> 00:44:59.935
um, and I think that that's a great source of,

00:44:59.935 --> 00:45:01.315
of information and thinking about,

00:45:01.315 --> 00:45:02.590
you know, working with this,

00:45:02.590 --> 00:45:05.770
this problem that I have and using those tools from that discipline

00:45:05.770 --> 00:45:08.950
to identify what those root causes might be. For sure.

00:45:08.950 --> 00:45:15.430
Um, so, the last one that I think we'll have time for.

00:45:15.430 --> 00:45:18.670
So, um, a lot of people are, I think,

00:45:18.670 --> 00:45:21.970
trying to figure out how to re-frame their own,

00:45:21.970 --> 00:45:24.850
um, their own questions and challenges.

00:45:24.850 --> 00:45:25.195
Yeah.

00:45:25.195 --> 00:45:31.059
So, um, perhaps we could answer most of these types of questions,

00:45:31.059 --> 00:45:35.455
um, if you could talk about how you re-framed the,

00:45:35.455 --> 00:45:39.250
you know, this issue of meeting nutritional needs of a growing population.

00:45:39.250 --> 00:45:41.365
Like, how- how did you re-frame that?

00:45:41.365 --> 00:45:44.140
Yeah. That's a great question.

00:45:44.140 --> 00:45:48.205
So, I think in, in a couple of ways.

00:45:48.205 --> 00:45:51.865
Um, one is that,

00:45:51.865 --> 00:45:55.060
uh, I see the problem, so,

00:45:55.060 --> 00:45:57.430
we- I've re-framed that problem as, um,

00:45:57.430 --> 00:46:03.440
the problem is that we need to maximize the productive capacity of our soils.

00:46:03.810 --> 00:46:08.350
Um, and that takes into account what we are actually doing to our soils,

00:46:08.350 --> 00:46:12.220
which is diminishing their viability over long-term.

00:46:12.220 --> 00:46:15.700
It's also inclusive of this problem that we need to

00:46:15.700 --> 00:46:20.230
maximize productivity in order to produce food for this growing population.

00:46:20.230 --> 00:46:22.030
So I re-framed it in that way,

00:46:22.030 --> 00:46:24.835
to be inclusive of both of those problems.

00:46:24.835 --> 00:46:27.025
There's a separate problem, um,

00:46:27.025 --> 00:46:34.795
that we've re-framed in a way on to identify people in their implicit needs, and the,

00:46:34.795 --> 00:46:37.165
the question that we, that I often ask

00:46:37.165 --> 00:46:40.790
of entrepreneurs and people who are working in foods specifically,

00:46:40.790 --> 00:46:43.945
um, or in agriculture is,

00:46:43.945 --> 00:46:48.369
um, how can we grow,

00:46:48.369 --> 00:46:54.725
produce, sell, and consume food that unlocks the potential of humans.

00:46:54.725 --> 00:47:02.130
I like that one because it really re-frames food as being just calories that feed

00:47:02.130 --> 00:47:10.660
people to food as being a tool to really unlock and tap into the potential of people.

00:47:10.660 --> 00:47:12.520
I think by thinking about it that way,

00:47:12.520 --> 00:47:14.470
it just really re-frames, like,

00:47:14.470 --> 00:47:18.385
how we think about the utility of food in a very different way.

00:47:18.385 --> 00:47:23.350
I think it begins to get at or encompass some of the really,

00:47:23.350 --> 00:47:26.050
um, terrible problems that were, that were existing,

00:47:26.050 --> 00:47:29.755
that exists in the system today with respect to food and people around,

00:47:29.755 --> 00:47:32.920
um, the skyrocketing obesity rates that we're seeing,

00:47:32.920 --> 00:47:36.820
the rates of type two diabetes that exists today, um,

00:47:36.820 --> 00:47:42.250
the inequities in the system that leads to a billion people on the planet,

00:47:42.250 --> 00:47:45.310
um, either starving or being malnourished.

00:47:45.310 --> 00:47:48.940
Um, and so, I think that,

00:47:48.940 --> 00:47:51.505
I think that if we can solve that problem, uh,

00:47:51.505 --> 00:47:53.350
we will be going,

00:47:53.350 --> 00:47:54.730
to focus on that as being the problem.

00:47:54.730 --> 00:47:59.200
I think that we'll go a long way in designing a food system that

00:47:59.200 --> 00:48:04.435
actually has people at its center and that is trying to make health be productive,

00:48:04.435 --> 00:48:09.040
um, and to help them realize their potential negotiate possible.

